  "pr_title": "feat(cli): add spsync doctor to check first-run setup",
  "pr_description": "## Goal\n\nAdd a `spsync doctor` command that tells a teammate which setup steps are missing and how to fix each one. Today a teammate finds these problems only when a pull or a push fails.\n\n## Summary\n\n`spsync doctor` checks the CLI version, the OneDrive folder under `~/Library/CloudStorage/`, Word for Mac, the macOS Automation permission to control Word, and the comment author name. Each check prints a pass or fail line. Each fail line includes one fix in plain language. The command only reports; it changes no settings.\n\nThe OneDrive folder, Word, and the Automation permission are required checks. In graph mode, the OneDrive check is optional, because the graph backend does not use OneDrive. The CLI version and the author name always pass. The author line shows the name, its source (the spsync setting, the macOS full name, or the macOS login name), and the command that changes it. When a required check fails, the command exits with code 3. The plugin uses this code to tell an incomplete setup apart from a CLI failure. `--json` prints the same results for the plugin.\n\nmacOS reports the Automation permission only when a process sends Word an Apple event. When Word is closed, the check starts Word hidden and in the background with `open -g -j`, sends `get name`, and quits Word after the check. When Word is already open, the check leaves Word open. Error -1743 means that macOS denies the permission. The first run can show the macOS permission prompt. The check waits for the answer, within the time limit that the plugin sets for one CLI run.\n\nThe checks live in a new module, `src/spsync/doctor.py`. The checks read the Mac through `SetupEnvironment`, so tests replace the file system and Word with fakes. `local.py` now exposes `onedrive_folders()` and `comment_author()`, so the local backend and `doctor` share one rule for each value. `spsync mode` gains `--json`; the plugin reads the author name from it. The README and the module list and dependency illustration in `CODING_STANDARDS.md` describe the new command.\n\nThis change does not touch docx reading or patching.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `uv run ruff format --check`, `uv run ruff check`, `uv run ty check`, `uv run anti-slop-python src tests`, and `uv run pytest` pass.\n- `tests/test_doctor.py` covers each check that passes and fails, with temporary folders for the file system and a fake Word probe. The tests also cover the optional OneDrive check in graph mode and the `complete` rule that sets the exit code.\n- `uv run pytest -m integration tests/test_doctor.py` passes against Word for Mac on a Mac that has the Automation permission.\n- Manual: `spsync doctor` and `spsync doctor --json` report every check as passing on a Mac with OneDrive and Word.\n- Manual check still needed: quit Word, run `spsync doctor`, and confirm that Word does not show a window and does not stay running after the check.\n\n## Issue\n\nRefs #10\n"
